## Fact sources

Some bundled data states facts about how a database engine behaves — which
algorithm it runs a schema change under, whether that change rewrites the table,
whether other sessions can keep writing. Those facts are established by the
engine vendors' own reference manuals, and the manuals are named here because a
fact worth shipping is worth attributing:

- MySQL 8.4 online-DDL behavior — MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual (Oracle),
  <https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/>
- The rule IDENTIFIERS of two external PostgreSQL catalogs — Supabase Splinter,
  <https://github.com/supabase/splinter>, and the PGLS `dblint` checks,
  <https://github.com/supabase-community/postgres-language-server>. Only the ids and
  the links are carried, in `rule-dedupe-rls.json`, so that the same fact is not
  reported twice once an adapter exists. That a catalog HAS a check under a given
  id is a fact; the wording of those checks is theirs and none of it appears here
- Squawk rule names and what each rule flags — Squawk's own rule documentation,
  <https://squawkhq.com/docs/rules>, and the binary itself: every name in
  `squawk-map.json` was verified against `squawk 2.61.0` rather than copied from
  the page, because a list read from documentation is a list of what the docs say,
  not of what the installed tool accepts
- PGLS `dblint` security rule names, their severities and their help links — the
  `postgrestools` binary itself, at 0.25.7. The tool runs its catalog as one SQL
  statement in which each rule carries a metadata header, so `pgls-map.json` was
  read out of a run rather than off a page. It was read twice, against differently
  shaped databases, because one reading cannot tell a fixed catalog from a
  coincidence. What the tool CALLS each rule and how it DESCRIBES it are its
  authors' prose and are not carried: every explanation in that file was written
  here

No text, wording, or table from those manuals is reproduced. Facts themselves
carry no license: the structure, the prose, and every explanation in the shipped
data files were written for this package, and a guard test holds each cited
source to its versioned manual page and each explanation to its own words.

## Bundled data

Every data artifact the package ships is recorded here before it is used, in this
shape:

    artifact · source URL · license (SPDX) · retrieved (ISO date)

The structure is mandatory rather than customary. A data file whose origin nobody
wrote down cannot be re-verified when the upstream facts change, and cannot be
defended if its license turns out to disallow redistribution. "We copied it from
somewhere" is not a provenance.

One artifact needs a sentence of its own. `incidents.json` CITES third-party
postmortems and reproduces none of them: every summary in it is written here from
the source, and the source is linked with the date somebody read it. So the file is
this project's own work under `CC0-1.0` like the rest, while the outages it
describes remain the accounts of the organizations that published them — which is
also why the retrieval date is a required field rather than a courtesy.

A data artifact the project writes itself is registered the same way, with its
source pointing at this repository. Such artifacts are released under `CC0-1.0`:
they are catalogs of plain facts — class and method names, and the reason each
one matters — and a fact table carries no attribution burden worth imposing on
anyone who reads or re-uses it. The surrounding code keeps the package's own
license; the data allowlist stays as narrow as it was.

